          body:
            "Start by pinning down the complaint.\n\n[[excessive-daytime-sleepiness|Excessive daytime sleepiness]] is an increased tendency to fall asleep: dozing when reading, watching television, as a passenger, or, dangerously, when driving. Fatigue is a lack of energy or a sense of exhaustion without actually falling asleep. Fatigue is common in depression, multiple sclerosis, and chronic illness. Patients use \"tired\" for both, so ask: \"Do you actually fall asleep?\"\n\nThen look for the common causes before the rare ones:\n\n- Insufficient sleep: by far the commonest cause. Ask about bedtimes and wake times on work days and free days. Sleeping much longer at weekends suggests a weekday sleep debt.\n- [[obstructive-sleep-apnea|Obstructive sleep apnea]]: loud snoring, pauses in breathing seen by a bed partner, waking with choking, unrefreshing sleep, morning headaches. Obesity, a large neck, and high blood pressure add to the suspicion. It is common and treatable.\n- Drugs and substances: sedating antihistamines, opioids, benzodiazepines and other hypnotics, some antidepressants and antiseizure medicines, dopamine agonists, and alcohol.\n- Shift work and circadian rhythm disorders, depression, and other medical and neurological illness.\n\nOnly when these are dealt with does a central disorder of hypersomnolence, such as [[narcolepsy|narcolepsy]] or [[idiopathic-hypersomnia|idiopathic hypersomnia]], come into focus.",
