          body:
            "[[narcolepsy|Narcolepsy]] usually begins in adolescence or early adulthood. Its features are:\n\n- [[excessive-daytime-sleepiness|Excessive daytime sleepiness]], with irresistible sleep episodes; short naps are often refreshing.\n- [[cataplexy|Cataplexy]]: sudden, brief episodes of muscle weakness on both sides of the body, triggered by emotion, most often laughter, joking, or surprise. The jaw sags, the head drops, the knees buckle, and the person may slump to the ground. Consciousness and memory are preserved throughout. Episodes last seconds to a couple of minutes and end with full, immediate recovery.\n- [[sleep-paralysis|Sleep paralysis]]: waking up unable to move for a short time.\n- [[hypnagogic-hallucination|Hypnagogic hallucinations]]: vivid, dreamlike experiences while falling asleep (hypnopompic when waking).\n- Broken night sleep.\n\nNarcolepsy type 1 is narcolepsy with cataplexy, low hypocretin in the cerebrospinal fluid, or both. It is caused by loss of the [[hypocretin|hypocretin]] (orexin) producing neurons in the lateral hypothalamus, thought to be autoimmune, with a strong association with the HLA-DQB1*06:02 allele. Narcolepsy type 2 has the sleepiness and the sleep test findings but no cataplexy, and hypocretin is normal or unmeasured. Type 2 is less well understood, and its test findings are not always reproducible on repeat testing.\n\nSleep paralysis and hypnagogic hallucinations on their own are common in healthy people, especially after sleep loss. They support narcolepsy only alongside the sleepiness.",
