          body:
            "The diagnosis of narcolepsy needs objective testing, done in a particular order and on a well-prepared patient.\n\n1. First make sure the patient is sleeping enough. A sleep diary, often with [[actigraphy|actigraphy]] (a wrist movement monitor), over the preceding weeks shows the real sleep schedule. Insufficient sleep and shifted sleep timing distort the tests.\n2. Review medicines. Drugs that suppress REM sleep, including many antidepressants, and sedating or stimulant drugs alter the results, so they are withdrawn beforehand where this is safe, with planning.\n3. Overnight [[polysomnography|polysomnography]] records brain waves, eye movements, muscle activity, breathing, oxygen, and leg movements. It shows that the patient had a full night's sleep before the next test and looks for another explanation, such as sleep apnea.\n4. The next day, a [[multiple-sleep-latency-test|multiple sleep latency test]] (MSLT) offers a series of daytime naps at set intervals in a dark, quiet room. It measures how quickly the patient falls asleep and whether REM sleep appears within minutes of falling asleep ([[sleep-onset-rem-period|sleep-onset REM periods]]). Short sleep latency with repeated early REM periods supports narcolepsy.\n\nMeasuring hypocretin in the cerebrospinal fluid by lumbar puncture is useful when the MSLT cannot be interpreted (for example, when REM-suppressing drugs cannot be stopped) or results are unclear. Low hypocretin establishes type 1.\n\nThe MSLT is only as good as the preparation. Sleep deprivation, shift work, untreated sleep apnea, and drug withdrawal can all produce early REM periods in people without narcolepsy, and an MSLT done without a preceding night of polysomnography can mislead.",
