            "Sleep runs in cycles of non-REM (NREM) and REM sleep through the night. Deep NREM sleep (slow-wave sleep) is concentrated in the first third of the night. REM sleep, the stage of vivid dreaming, gets longer with each cycle and dominates the last third, toward morning.\n\nIn normal REM sleep the brain actively paralyzes almost all skeletal muscles except the eye muscles and the diaphragm. This REM atonia keeps us from acting out our dreams.\n\nThat gives a simple rule for behavior in sleep:\n\n- Events in the first hours after falling asleep suggest a disorder of deep NREM sleep: an [[nrem-parasomnia|NREM parasomnia]] such as [[sleepwalking|sleepwalking]] or [[sleep-terror|sleep terrors]].\n- Events in the second half of the night, with dream recall, suggest [[rem-sleep-behavior-disorder|REM sleep behavior disorder]].\n- Events at any time, many times a night, brief and identical each time, suggest seizures.",
