            "[[rem-sleep-behavior-disorder|REM sleep behavior disorder]] (RBD) is dream enactment caused by loss of REM atonia. Typical features:\n\n- Talking, shouting, swearing, punching, or kicking in sleep, often in the later part of the night.\n- Vivid, often unpleasant dreams of being attacked or chased, which the patient recalls on waking and which match the movements.\n- Injuries to the patient or bed partner, and falls from bed.\n- Most often in adults over 50, more often recognized in men.\n\nDiagnosis needs a history of dream enactment plus video [[polysomnography|polysomnography]] showing [[rem-sleep-without-atonia|REM sleep without atonia]], muscle activity during REM sleep where there should be none. The video also helps rule out mimics such as sleep apnea causing arousals with movements, and seizures.\n\nRBD can be triggered or unmasked by antidepressants, and it occurs in narcolepsy type 1. When it has no other cause it is called idiopathic (or isolated) RBD, and this is where its importance lies: idiopathic RBD is a strong early marker of a [[synucleinopathy|synucleinopathy]]. Most older adults with idiopathic RBD go on, often after years, to develop Parkinson disease, [[dementia-with-lewy-bodies|dementia with Lewy bodies]], or [[multiple-system-atrophy|multiple system atrophy]].\n\nManagement starts with safety: moving furniture and sharp objects away, padding the floor, and sometimes sleeping apart. Medication can reduce the episodes. Follow-up looks for early signs of parkinsonism, cognitive change, and autonomic failure.",
