          body:
            "[[nrem-parasomnia|NREM parasomnias]] arise from deep NREM sleep, so they cluster in the first part of the night.\n\n- [[sleepwalking|Sleepwalking]]: getting up and walking or doing complex things, eyes open but glassy, with little memory afterward.\n- [[sleep-terror|Sleep terrors]]: sitting up suddenly with a scream, looking terrified, with racing heart, fast breathing, and sweating; inconsolable, hard to wake, and amnesic in the morning.\n- Confusional arousals: waking partly, confused and slow, without leaving the bed.\n\nThey are common in children, usually outgrown, and often run in families. Sleep deprivation, fever, stress, and anything that fragments sleep (including sleep apnea) can provoke them. Most children need only reassurance and a safe environment; adults with new or dangerous episodes deserve evaluation.\n\nNocturnal frontal lobe seizures, now often called [[sleep-related-hypermotor-epilepsy|sleep-related hypermotor epilepsy]], are the important mimic. They are focal seizures, often with hyperkinetic movements, that arise mostly from NREM sleep. Clues:\n\n- Brief, usually under a couple of minutes.\n- Highly stereotyped: the same sequence every time.\n- Clustered: several in one night, at any time of night.\n- Abrupt onset with thrashing, kicking, bicycling, or stiff, twisted postures, sometimes with a cry.\n- Onset at any age, often adolescence or young adulthood.\n\nThe scalp EEG is often normal, even during an event, because the frontal lobe source is deep. Video recording of a typical event, with [[video-eeg|video-EEG]] or video polysomnography with extended EEG, is the key test, and a home video is a useful start.",
