          body:
            "[[restless-legs-syndrome|Restless legs syndrome]] (RLS, also called Willis-Ekbom disease) is diagnosed from the history. The International Restless Legs Syndrome Study Group criteria require all of these:\n\n1. An urge to move the legs, usually with uncomfortable sensations in them.\n2. The urge begins or worsens during rest or inactivity, such as sitting or lying down.\n3. Movement, such as walking or stretching, partly or fully relieves it, at least while the movement continues.\n4. It occurs only, or is worse, in the evening or at night.\n5. It is not explained solely by another condition, such as leg cramps, positional discomfort, arthritis, leg swelling, or habitual foot tapping.\n\n[[periodic-limb-movements|Periodic limb movements]] in sleep are common in RLS and support it, but they are not required.\n\nLook for causes and aggravating factors: iron deficiency, kidney failure, pregnancy, and neuropathy, and drugs such as sedating antihistamines, dopamine-blocking antiemetics and antipsychotics, and many antidepressants. Check iron stores (ferritin and transferrin saturation) in everyone with clinically significant RLS. Iron replacement helps when stores are low, and sleep guidelines use a higher threshold for treating than the usual definition of iron deficiency.\n\nDopamine agonists relieve RLS in the short term, but long-term use often leads to [[augmentation|augmentation]]: symptoms begin earlier in the day, become more intense, appear sooner at rest, and spread to the arms. The 2024 American Academy of Sleep Medicine guideline therefore suggests against their standard use, and augmentation needs specialist review rather than a higher dose.",
